Chapter 5. Information Geometry of Wildfire Risk

5.1 Fisher–Rao Metric on the Parameter Manifold

Let \boldsymbol{\theta} \in \Theta \subset \mathbb{R}^p be environmental parameters (fuel load, wind, moisture, etc.). The 1D PDE defines a deterministic forecast map \mathcal{F} : \Theta \to \mathcal{X} (e.g., burnt area, peak temperature). Observations are \mathbf{y} = \mathcal{F}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) + \boldsymbol{\eta}, with \boldsymbol{\eta} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 I). The Fisher metric is

g_{ij}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \frac{1}{\sigma^2} \sum_k \frac{\partial \mathcal{F}_k}{\partial \theta_i} \frac{\partial \mathcal{F}_k}{\partial \theta_j} = \frac{1}{\sigma^2} (J^{\mathsf T} J)_{ij}, \tag{6}

where J is the Jacobian of \mathcal{F}.

5.2 Why Curvature Fails as an Early‑Warning Signal

Because g is the pullback under \mathcal{F} of the flat Euclidean metric on \mathcal{X}, the intrinsic Riemannian curvature of (\Theta, g) vanishes identically wherever J has full rank. Near a fold, where J drops rank, the metric degenerates; any finite curvature computed nearby is a coordinate artifact, not a geometric invariant. Thus scalar curvature cannot provide a reliable divergence signal. Instead, one must use the spectrum of g directly.

5.3 Singular‑Value Degeneracy at the Fold

Assume the parameter manifold is partitioned into a dangerous direction s = R_f - R_f^* and regular directions \mathbf{u} \in \mathbb{R}^{p-1}. Near the critical manifold \mathcal{M}^* = \{s = 0\}, the forecast map generically takes the normal form

\mathcal{F}(s, \mathbf{u}) = \mathcal{G}(\mathbf{u}) + \mathbf{v} \, s^2 + O(s^3),

where \mathbf{v} \neq 0 is transverse to the range of D\mathcal{G}. The Jacobian is J = D\mathcal{G} + 2s \, \mathbf{v} e_s^{\mathsf T} + O(s^2). Because the column corresponding to s is O(s) while the remaining block is full rank, the smallest singular value of J satisfies

\sigma_{\min}(J) = \|\mathbf{v}\| \, |s| + O(s^2).

Consequently the smallest eigenvalue of the Fisher metric behaves as

\lambda_{\min}(g) = \frac{\|\mathbf{v}\|^2}{\sigma^2} \, s^2 + O(s^3) \quad \longrightarrow \quad 0 \;\; \text{as } s \to 0,

and the condition number diverges like \kappa(g) \sim C / s^2. This is a rigorous, coordinate‑free signal that the parameters are approaching a fold — it requires only linear algebra on the Jacobian, not curvature.

Proposition 3 (Condition‑number blow‑up). For a generic fold with the above normal form, the condition number \kappa(g) of the Fisher metric diverges as s^{-2} when the dangerous parameter approaches the critical value.

Proof. Let J(s,\mathbf{u}) = D\mathcal{G}(\mathbf{u}) + 2s \mathbf{v} e_s^{\mathsf T} + O(s^2). Since D\mathcal{G} has full rank p-1 and \mathbf{v} \notin \operatorname{range}(D\mathcal{G}), the perturbed matrix has a singular value that crosses zero linearly with slope \|\mathbf{v}\|. By the implicit function theorem applied to the singular value problem, \sigma_{\min}(J) = \|\mathbf{v}\| |s| + O(s^2). Then \lambda_{\min}(g) = \sigma_{\min}(J)^2 / \sigma^2, and \kappa(g) = \lambda_{\max} / \lambda_{\min}. Because the largest eigenvalue remains bounded away from zero, \kappa(g) = C / s^2 + O(1/|s|). ∎

5.4 Early‑Warning Algorithm

Monitor the empirical condition number \kappa(\hat g) estimated from the ensemble of parameter particles (see Chapter 7). A sustained increase in \kappa(\hat g), or a collapse of the smallest eigenvalue, signals that the landscape is drifting toward the critical manifold. This diagnostic is well‑defined arbitrarily close to the fold and relies purely on the Jacobian of the forward map; no empirical tuning is required.

