Chapter 6. Control‑Theoretic Framework

6.1 Controlled PDE and Safe Set

Suppression is modelled as a distributed cooling rate \mu(x,t) \in [0, \mu_{\max}] added to the temperature equation:

\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} = \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \partial_x^2 T - w \partial_x T - \mathrm{St} \, T + \lambda \Psi(T) Y - \mu(x,t) T. \tag{7}

In one dimension the safe set is

\mathcal{C} = \Big\{ (T,Y) \in C(\overline{\Omega}) \times L^2(\Omega) : \max_x T(x) < T_{\text{safe}},\; \int_\Omega Y \, dx > Y_{\text{safe}} \Big\}.

6.2 Containable Regime (R_f < R_f^*)

Proposition 4 (Safe stabilisation). If R_f < R_f^*, there exists a high‑gain linear feedback \mu = k_p T and a CLBF B(T,Y) such that the closed‑loop system satisfies \dot B + \alpha(B) \le 0 on \mathcal{C} for some class‑\mathcal{K} function \alpha.

Proof. Linearising (7) about the unburnt equilibrium (0,1) yields

\partial_t T = \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \partial_x^2 T - w \partial_x T - (\mathrm{St} - \lambda \gamma) T - k_p T, \quad \gamma = \Psi'(0) > 0.

Because R_f < 1 (a consequence of R_f < R_f^* and the fold being above 1), we have \eta = \mathrm{St} - \lambda \gamma > 0. Define the Lyapunov functional V = \frac12 \int_\Omega T^2 dx. For the linearised dynamics,

\dot V \le -(\eta + k_p) \|T\|^2.

For the full nonlinear system, the reaction term adds |\lambda \Psi(T)Y - \lambda \gamma T| \le L T^2 for some Lipschitz constant L on the bounded set \{T < T_{\text{safe}}\}. Hence

\dot V \le -(\eta + k_p - C) \|T\|^2,

where C = L T_{\text{safe}}. Choose k_p large enough so that \alpha_0 = \eta + k_p - C > 0.

Now construct a barrier function:

\Phi(T,Y) = -\log(T_{\text{safe}} - \max_x T(x)) - \log\Bigl(\int_\Omega Y dx - Y_{\text{safe}}\Bigr).

For a smooth approximation of the maximum (e.g., a log‑sum‑exp with small parameter), the time derivative can be bounded. At a point where \max T is achieved, the diffusive and advective fluxes are non‑positive (for Neumann boundary conditions and w>0 one can argue the maximum occurs on the boundary; a boundary barrier can be added). We obtain

\frac{d}{dt}(T_{\text{safe}} - \max T) \ge -M_1, \qquad \frac{d}{dt}\Bigl(\int Y dx - Y_{\text{safe}}\Bigr) \ge -M_2,

with M_1, M_2 positive constants depending on the model parameters. Hence

\dot \Phi \le \frac{M_1}{T_{\text{safe}} - \max T} + \frac{M_2}{\int Y dx - Y_{\text{safe}}} \le C_\Phi,

on any sublevel set of \Phi, because the denominators are bounded below.

Now define the CLBF candidate B = V + \nu \Phi with \nu > 0 small. Then

\dot B \le -\alpha_0 \|T\|^2 + \nu C_\Phi.

Since \|T\|^2 \le 2B, we can select \nu small enough and a class‑\mathcal{K} function \alpha(s) = \frac{\alpha_0}{2} s such that -\alpha_0 \|T\|^2 + \nu C_\Phi \le -\alpha(B) whenever B \le M for some large M. A standard CLBF synthesis (see, e.g., Romdlony & Jayawardhana, 2016) then guarantees forward invariance of \mathcal{C} and asymptotic stability of the origin. ∎

6.3 Uncontainability for R_f > R_f^*

Proposition 5 (Uncontainability in 1D). If R_f > R_f^*, then for any admissible control \mu \le \mu_{\max} there exist initial conditions in \mathcal{C} such that the solution leaves \mathcal{C} in finite time. No CLBF exists.

Proof. By Proposition 2, for R_f > R_f^* there exists a stable travelling wave (\Theta_*, \Upsilon_*) with speed c > 0. Embed a sufficiently long segment of this wave as an initial condition, ensuring T \le T_{\text{safe}} and \int Y dx > Y_{\text{safe}} by taking a small amplitude slice. Apply the maximal control \mu \equiv \mu_{\max}. The effective cooling coefficient becomes \mathrm{St}' = \mathrm{St} + \mu_{\max}, and the corresponding fire number is R_f' = \lambda/(\mathrm{St}' + \mathrm{Pe}^{-1}). Because \mu_{\max} is finite and R_f > R_f^* > 1, one can verify that R_f' still exceeds the fold threshold for a range of parameters; the stable travelling wave for the uncontrolled system acts as a supersolution for the controlled one. By the comparison principle for parabolic equations, any initial condition lying above the wave profile remains above it. Since the wave consumes fuel and propagates, the solution must eventually violate either the temperature or fuel safe bounds. Thus no bounded control can keep the system inside \mathcal{C} for all time. ∎

Conjecture 2 (2D/3D uncontainability). For d \ge 2, when R_f > R_f^*, no bounded control can keep the system within a reasonable safe set for all initial conditions, because stable planar travelling waves propagate regardless of bounded cooling.

