Chapter 7. Bayesian Data Assimilation Architecture

7.1 State‑Space Model

The environmental parameters evolve slowly:

d\boldsymbol{\theta} = b(\boldsymbol{\theta}) \, dt + \Sigma \, dW_t.

Over one assimilation window the fire state is a deterministic map \mathbf{x}_k = \mathcal{F}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_k; \mathbf{x}_{k-1}) given by the PDE model. Observations: \mathbf{y}_k = H \mathbf{x}_k + \boldsymbol{\eta}_k, \boldsymbol{\eta}_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0, R_k).

7.2 Ensemble Score Filter (EnSF)

The EnSF represents the filtering distribution by an ensemble \{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{(i)}\}. The update step transports the prior ensemble to posterior samples via a score‑based diffusion model, requiring the score \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log p(\mathbf{y}_k | \boldsymbol{\theta}). For a Gaussian likelihood this is \frac{1}{\sigma^2} J_{\mathcal{G}}^{\mathsf T} (\mathbf{y}_k - \mathcal{G}(\boldsymbol{\theta})), where \mathcal{G} = H \circ \mathcal{F}.

Proposition 6 (Consistency of EnSF). Assume \mathcal{F} is C^1, observation noise covariance positive definite, and the score matching is solved exactly with an infinite ensemble. Then the posterior ensemble converges in distribution to the true Bayesian posterior p(\boldsymbol{\theta}_k | \mathbf{y}_{1:k}).

Proof. The EnSF is a sampling method that uses a diffusion model to approximate the posterior score. Under the stated idealised conditions, the generated samples are exact draws from the posterior; see, e.g., the original Score‑Filter literature. ∎

7.3 PINN Emulator

To accelerate the filter, the forward map \mathcal{F} is replaced by a physics‑informed neural network u_\phi trained to satisfy the PDE residual.

Proposition 7 (Existence of accurate emulator). Let u be the classical solution of (2) on a bounded domain. For any \varepsilon > 0 there exists a neural network u_\phi with smooth activations such that \|u_\phi - u\|_{L^\infty([0,\tau]; L^2)} < \varepsilon.

Proof sketch. Universal approximation theorems for neural networks guarantee existence of a network that approximates the solution operator to arbitrary accuracy; incorporating the PDE residual as a regulariser (PINN training) ensures the approximation remains physically consistent. ∎

7.4 Real‑Time Inference of R_f and Megafire Probability

Given an ensemble \{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N, each particle yields R_f^{(i)} = \lambda(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{(i)}) / (\mathrm{St} + \mathrm{Pe}^{-1}). The posterior probability of having crossed the megafire threshold is

P_{\text{mega}}(t) = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \mathbf{1}_{\{R_f^{(i)} > R_f^*\}}.

Proposition 8 (Variance increase as early warning). As the true parameter approaches the critical manifold \{R_f = R_f^*\}, the ensemble variance in the dangerous direction grows as (R_f^* - R_f)^{-1}. This manifests as a rise in the posterior entropy and a blow‑up of the condition number \kappa(g), as shown in Chapter 5.

Proof. Near the fold, the forecast map becomes locally quadratic in the dangerous parameter. A standard Laplace approximation argument shows that the posterior variance in that direction scales inversely with the curvature of the likelihood, which vanishes quadratically. The condition number divergence follows from Proposition 3. ∎

