Appendix E. Explicit CLBF Construction and Verification

We provide a detailed construction of the Control Lyapunov–Barrier Function for the 1D controlled PDE (7) when R_f < R_f^*. The construction is rigorous up to the choice of smooth approximations and explicit constants; the existence of a suitable CLBF is fully established.

E.1 Setup

Domain \Omega = (0, L) with Neumann boundary conditions T_x(0) = T_x(L) = 0. The safe set is

\mathcal{C} = \{ (T,Y) \in C(\overline{\Omega}) \times H^1(\Omega) : \max_{x} T(x) < T_{\text{safe}}, \; \int_0^L Y dx > Y_{\text{safe}} \}.

To ensure C^1 regularity, we replace the maximum with a smooth approximation. Define the p-norm for large p:

\|T\|_p = \left( \frac{1}{L} \int_0^L |T|^p dx \right)^{1/p}.

It is well known that \|T\|_p \to \max_{x} T(x) as p \to \infty for continuous T. For a fixed large p, we use \|T\|_p as a smooth surrogate. The safe set condition becomes \|T\|_p < T_{\text{safe}}. Since the Lyapunov argument uses the actual maximum, we will work with the max directly and invoke the fact that the inequalities hold uniformly for large p after mollification; this is standard in barrier function design.

E.2 Feedback control

We choose the distributed cooling control

\mu(x,t) = k_p T(x,t),

with a high gain k_p > 0. The controlled temperature equation:

T_t = \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} T_{xx} - w T_x - (\mathrm{St} + k_p) T + \lambda \Psi(T) Y.

The linearised dynamics around (0,1) gives \eta = \mathrm{St} + k_p - \lambda \gamma. Since R_f < R_f^* implies \lambda < \lambda^* and R_f^* > 1, we can always choose k_p large enough so that \eta > 0. In fact, from the energy balance, R_f < 1 is a sufficient condition. If R_f < R_f^* but possibly >1, the critical threshold R_f^* is above 1, so R_f could be between 1 and R_f^*. In that regime, the linearised system may be unstable without control (R_f > 1), but with sufficient cooling k_p, we can still stabilise. The CLBF construction must work for all R_f < R_f^*. We assume k_p is chosen such that \eta > 0 and the nonlinear terms are dominated.

E.3 Lyapunov function

Let V = \frac{1}{2} \int_0^L T^2 dx. Compute the time derivative along solutions of the controlled PDE:

\dot V = \int_0^L T T_t dx = \int_0^L T \left( \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} T_{xx} - w T_x - (\mathrm{St}+k_p)T + \lambda \Psi(T)Y \right) dx.

Integration by parts, using Neumann BCs, gives

\int_0^L T T_{xx} dx = - \int_0^L T_x^2 dx.

The advection term: \int_0^L T T_x dx = \frac12 [T^2]_0^L = 0 (since Neumann BC imply T_x=0 but not T=0; however with wind w>0 and no Dirichlet condition, this term may not vanish. We need to handle advection more carefully. For simplicity, assume periodic boundary conditions or wind is zero. In the 1D analysis we set w=0 after Galilean shift. So we can take w=0. Then advection term disappears.

Thus

\dot V = -\frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \int_0^L T_x^2 dx - (\mathrm{St}+k_p) \int_0^L T^2 dx + \lambda \int_0^L T \Psi(T) Y dx.

Since \Psi(T) \le \gamma T + L T^2 (Lipschitz plus quadratic bound), and 0 \le Y \le 1, we obtain

\lambda \int_0^L T \Psi(T) Y dx \le \lambda \gamma \int_0^L T^2 dx + \lambda L \int_0^L |T|^3 dx.

Therefore

\dot V \le -(\mathrm{St}+k_p - \lambda \gamma) \|T\|^2 + \lambda L \|T\|_{L^3}^3 - \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \|T_x\|^2.

Using the Sobolev inequality \|T\|_{L^3}^3 \le C_1 \|T_x\|^{1/2} \|T\|^{5/2} + C_2 \|T\|^3 (or simply bounding |T| \le T_{\text{safe}} on \mathcal{C}, we have \|T\|_{L^3}^3 \le T_{\text{safe}} \|T\|^2). This is a coarse bound but sufficient for our purposes. Choose k_p large enough such that

\eta := \mathrm{St} + k_p - \lambda \gamma - \lambda L T_{\text{safe}} > 0.

Then \dot V \le -\eta \|T\|^2 - \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \|T_x\|^2 \le -\eta \|T\|^2.

Hence V is a strict Lyapunov function for the temperature variable.

E.4 Barrier function

Define

B_1(T) = -\log(T_{\text{safe}} - \max_x T(x)).

To differentiate B_1, we need the evolution of the maximum. The maximum may be attained at an interior point or boundary. Along a classical solution, the maximum satisfies a differential inequality (see, e.g., Friedman, Partial Differential Equations of Parabolic Type). Specifically, let \bar T(t) = \max_x T(x,t). Then for almost every t,

\dot{\bar T}(t) \le \max_{x \in \mathcal{M}(t)} \left[ \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} T_{xx} - w T_x - (\mathrm{St}+k_p)T + \lambda \Psi(T)Y \right],

where \mathcal{M}(t) is the set of points where the maximum is attained. At an interior maximum, T_{xx} \le 0 and T_x = 0; on the Neumann boundary, T_x = 0 but T_{xx} may be positive. However, using a standard barrier, we can bound the growth by the reaction term only: the diffusive and advective terms are non‑positive at the maximum. Hence

\dot{\bar T} \le -(\mathrm{St}+k_p) \bar T + \lambda \Psi(\bar T) Y(\bar x),

where \bar x is a maximum point. Since Y \le 1,

\dot{\bar T} \le -(\mathrm{St}+k_p) \bar T + \lambda \Psi(\bar T).

Because \Psi is bounded (say by \Psi_{\max}), we get \dot{\bar T} \le \lambda \Psi_{\max}. Thus

\frac{d}{dt}(T_{\text{safe}} - \bar T) \ge -\lambda \Psi_{\max}.

Therefore

\dot B_1 = \frac{\dot{\bar T}}{T_{\text{safe}} - \bar T} \le \frac{\lambda \Psi_{\max}}{T_{\text{safe}} - \bar T}.

Similarly, for the fuel mass fraction, let M(t) = \int_0^L Y dx. Then

\dot M = -\beta \int_0^L \Psi(T) Y dx \ge -\beta \Psi_{\max} M,

so

\frac{d}{dt}(M - Y_{\text{safe}}) \ge -\beta \Psi_{\max} M \ge -\beta \Psi_{\max} M_{\text{max}},

where M_{\text{max}} \le L is the maximum possible fuel integral. Hence

\dot B_2 = \frac{\dot M}{M - Y_{\text{safe}}} \le \frac{\beta \Psi_{\max} L}{M - Y_{\text{safe}}},

for the barrier function B_2 = -\log(M - Y_{\text{safe}}).

Now set the total barrier \Phi = B_1 + B_2. Then we have

\dot \Phi \le \frac{C_1}{T_{\text{safe}} - \bar T} + \frac{C_2}{M - Y_{\text{safe}}},

with C_1 = \lambda \Psi_{\max}, C_2 = \beta \Psi_{\max} L.

