Appendix F. Bayesian Data Assimilation and Fisher Metric Details

F.1 Ensemble Score Filter (EnSF) Update

The EnSF algorithm of Bao et al. (2023) proceeds as follows. Let the prior ensemble at time t_k be \{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k|k-1}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N. The observation is \mathbf{y}_k. The posterior distribution is p(\boldsymbol{\theta} | \mathbf{y}_{1:k}) \propto p(\mathbf{y}_k | \boldsymbol{\theta}) p(\boldsymbol{\theta} | \mathbf{y}_{1:k-1}).

Define the score of the likelihood: \mathbf{s}_{\text{like}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log p(\mathbf{y}_k | \boldsymbol{\theta}). For Gaussian noise \boldsymbol{\eta} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, R_k), with \mathbf{y}_k = \mathcal{G}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) + \boldsymbol{\eta}, we have

\mathbf{s}_{\text{like}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = J_{\mathcal{G}}(\boldsymbol{\theta})^{\mathsf T} R_k^{-1} (\mathbf{y}_k - \mathcal{G}(\boldsymbol{\theta})),

where J_{\mathcal{G}} is the Jacobian of the observation operator \mathcal{G} = H \circ \mathcal{F}.

The EnSF uses a diffusion model to sample from the posterior. The reverse‑time stochastic differential equation is

d\boldsymbol{\theta}_\tau = \left[ \mathbf{f}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_\tau, \tau) - g(\tau)^2 \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_\tau} \log p(\boldsymbol{\theta}_\tau | \mathbf{y}_{1:k}) \right] d\tau + g(\tau) d\mathbf{W}_\tau,

with appropriate score function. By Bayes’ rule, the posterior score decomposes as

\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log p(\boldsymbol{\theta} | \mathbf{y}_{1:k}) = \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log p(\boldsymbol{\theta} | \mathbf{y}_{1:k-1}) + \mathbf{s}_{\text{like}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}).

The prior score is estimated from the ensemble using score‑matching. The algorithm then discretises the reverse SDE and evolves samples from the prior to the posterior. Under the assumptions of exact score matching and infinite ensemble, the samples are exact draws from the posterior. This establishes Proposition 6.

F.2 Fisher Metric and Condition Number Monitoring

The Fisher information matrix for the likelihood p(\mathbf{y}|\boldsymbol{\theta}) at the current estimate is

g(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{y}|\boldsymbol{\theta}} \left[ \mathbf{s}_{\text{like}} \mathbf{s}_{\text{like}}^{\mathsf T} \right].

For Gaussian noise, g(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = J_{\mathcal{G}}(\boldsymbol{\theta})^{\mathsf T} R^{-1} J_{\mathcal{G}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}). In practice, we use the ensemble to estimate the Jacobian via finite differences or by training a local surrogate.

At each assimilation step, we compute the empirical Fisher matrix \hat g = \frac{1}{N-1} \sum_i (\mathbf{s}^{(i)} - \bar{\mathbf{s}})(\mathbf{s}^{(i)} - \bar{\mathbf{s}})^{\mathsf T} where \mathbf{s}^{(i)} = J_{\mathcal{G}}^{(i){\mathsf T}} R^{-1} (\mathbf{y} - \mathcal{G}(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{(i)})). The condition number \kappa(\hat g) is the ratio of largest to smallest eigenvalue. As shown in Appendix D, this condition number diverges as s^{-2} when approaching the fold. We monitor \kappa(\hat g) over time; a rapid increase indicates the ensemble is approaching the critical manifold, providing an early warning.

F.3 PINN Emulator

To accelerate the filter, we replace the expensive PDE solution \mathcal{F} with a physics‑informed neural network u_\phi(\mathbf{x},t;\boldsymbol{\theta}) that takes the parameters \boldsymbol{\theta} as input. The network is trained offline to minimise the PDE residual and match initial/boundary conditions. Proposition 7 asserts that for any \varepsilon>0 there exists a network that approximates the true solution to within \varepsilon in the L^\infty(L^2) norm. This is a consequence of the universal approximation theorem for neural networks and the stability of the PDE. In practice, the error of the PINN contributes to the observation error budget, which is accounted for in the filter's noise covariance.

With the PINN emulator, the Jacobian J_{\mathcal{G}} can be computed efficiently via automatic differentiation. The entire data assimilation pipeline then runs in near real time.

